1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:05,220
Put one of the largest stars we know in place of the Sun, and Earth wouldn't orbit it.

2
00:00:05,220 --> 00:00:06,971
Earth would be deep inside it.

3
00:00:06,971 --> 00:00:07,788
So would Mars.

4
00:00:07,788 --> 00:00:08,780
So would Jupiter.

5
00:00:08,780 --> 00:00:13,392
A passenger jet flying around it nonstop would need more than a thousand years.

6
00:00:13,392 --> 00:00:15,026
And here's the strange part.

7
00:00:15,026 --> 00:00:18,237
Astronomers can't agree on which star holds the record.

8
00:00:18,237 --> 00:00:21,448
And one of the most famous giants may have just shrunk.

9
00:00:22,147 --> 00:00:27,357
So let's grow a star, step by step, from our Sun to the biggest monster ever measured.

10
00:00:27,357 --> 00:00:38,745
By the end, you'll know how we measure something that looks like a single dot, why a star can't grow forever, and why the biggest stars in the universe are, in a strange way, almost empty.

11
00:00:39,445 --> 00:00:41,344
The Largest Star versus the Sun.

12
00:00:42,044 --> 00:00:43,192
Start with our Sun.

13
00:00:43,192 --> 00:00:46,215
It's about one point four million kilometers wide.

14
00:00:46,215 --> 00:00:48,633
A hundred and nine Earths, side by side.

15
00:00:48,633 --> 00:00:53,106
Hollow it out, and you could pour in about one point three million Earths.

16
00:00:53,106 --> 00:00:59,031
Now imagine a passenger jet cruising at nine hundred kilometers an hour, flying nonstop around it.

17
00:00:59,031 --> 00:01:02,477
By our calculation, one lap takes about two hundred days.

18
00:01:02,477 --> 00:01:03,686
Almost seven months.

19
00:01:03,686 --> 00:01:05,076
Hold on to that number.

20
00:01:05,076 --> 00:01:06,406
It's about to explode.

21
00:01:07,106 --> 00:01:07,654
Step one.

22
00:01:07,654 --> 00:01:12,954
Betelgeuse, the red shoulder of Orion, roughly five hundred and fifty light years away.

23
00:01:12,954 --> 00:01:15,573
You can see it tonight without a telescope.

24
00:01:15,573 --> 00:01:18,680
It's around seven hundred times wider than the Sun.

25
00:01:18,680 --> 00:01:26,903
Put it in the Sun's place, and its surface would reach past Mercury, past Venus, past Earth, past Mars, and out into the asteroid belt.

26
00:01:27,603 --> 00:01:34,763
And in twenty twenty five, astronomers using the Gemini North telescope finally spotted something long suspected.

27
00:01:34,763 --> 00:01:41,099
A small companion star, circling Betelgeuse about four times farther out than Earth is from the Sun.

28
00:01:41,099 --> 00:01:44,330
That's barely outside Betelgeuse's swollen surface.

29
00:01:44,330 --> 00:01:45,850
The companion is doomed.

30
00:01:45,850 --> 00:01:50,729
Within about ten thousand years, it's expected to spiral in and be swallowed.

31
00:01:51,429 --> 00:01:53,516
Now fly our jet around Betelgeuse.

32
00:01:53,516 --> 00:01:55,050
Not seven months anymore.

33
00:01:55,050 --> 00:01:57,321
About three hundred and ninety years.

34
00:01:57,321 --> 00:02:04,933
If you had taken off in the sixteen thirties, around the time Galileo was put on trial, you would only be landing about now.

35
00:02:05,633 --> 00:02:07,322
Betelgeuse is also restless.

36
00:02:07,322 --> 00:02:12,450
In late twenty nineteen, it dimmed so much that people noticed it with the naked eye.

37
00:02:12,450 --> 00:02:14,923
Some wondered if it was about to explode.

38
00:02:14,923 --> 00:02:25,602
Hubble later showed what really happened: the star had blasted a huge chunk of its surface into space, and that material cooled into a dust cloud that blocked part of its light.

39
00:02:25,602 --> 00:02:31,454
Betelgeuse will explode one day, as a supernova, probably within the next hundred thousand years.

40
00:02:31,454 --> 00:02:36,581
When it does, it should shine in our sky bright enough to see in daylight, for weeks.

41
00:02:37,281 --> 00:02:37,834
But wait.

42
00:02:37,834 --> 00:02:41,272
Apart from the Sun, every star is just a point of light.

43
00:02:41,272 --> 00:02:43,788
So how do you measure the width of a dot?

44
00:02:43,788 --> 00:02:45,262
There are two main ways.

45
00:02:45,262 --> 00:02:47,778
The first is to link telescopes together.

46
00:02:47,778 --> 00:02:53,119
Combine the light from telescopes spread far apart, and they act like one giant mirror.

47
00:02:53,119 --> 00:03:00,976
That's how the European Southern Observatory's Very Large Telescope Interferometer has resolved the actual disks of giant stars.

48
00:03:00,976 --> 00:03:02,695
The second way is a formula.

49
00:03:02,695 --> 00:03:11,473
If you know how much light a star really gives off, and how hot its surface is, physics tells you how much surface it needs to shine like that.

50
00:03:11,473 --> 00:03:15,648
A cool, red star that's still blindingly bright has only one option.

51
00:03:15,648 --> 00:03:16,998
It has to be enormous.

52
00:03:17,698 --> 00:03:18,675
There's a catch.

53
00:03:18,675 --> 00:03:20,872
Both methods depend on the distance.

54
00:03:20,872 --> 00:03:23,924
Get the distance wrong, and the size is wrong too.

55
00:03:23,924 --> 00:03:30,333
UY Scuti was once crowned the largest known star, at around seventeen hundred times the width of the Sun.

56
00:03:30,333 --> 00:03:37,475
Then the Gaia space telescope measured its distance more precisely, and the estimate dropped to roughly nine hundred.

57
00:03:37,475 --> 00:03:41,015
It lost almost half its width, on paper, almost overnight.

58
00:03:41,715 --> 00:03:43,451
So these stars are gigantic.

59
00:03:43,451 --> 00:03:44,629
Here's the paradox.

60
00:03:44,629 --> 00:03:46,365
They're also almost nothing.

61
00:03:46,365 --> 00:03:50,085
Betelgeuse holds roughly eighteen times the mass of the Sun.

62
00:03:50,085 --> 00:04:01,679
But spread that over a ball seven hundred times wider, and its average density comes out, by our calculation, about sixteen thousand times thinner than the air you're breathing right now.

63
00:04:01,679 --> 00:04:03,849
Its outer layers are thinner still.

64
00:04:03,849 --> 00:04:11,661
Fly a spaceship into Betelgeuse, and for a very long way, you'd be crossing a hot, glowing fog, thinner than any air on Earth.

65
00:04:12,361 --> 00:04:17,226
Which means the biggest star and the heaviest star are not the same thing at all.

66
00:04:17,226 --> 00:04:25,334
The heaviest star known, R one thirty six a one, in a neighboring galaxy, packs somewhere around two hundred times the mass of the Sun.

67
00:04:25,334 --> 00:04:28,757
Yet it's only about thirty five times wider than the Sun.

68
00:04:28,757 --> 00:04:31,760
Next to Betelgeuse, it's a tiny blue-white marble.

69
00:04:31,760 --> 00:04:33,562
Big stars are puffed-up stars.

70
00:04:33,562 --> 00:04:36,084
Heavy stars are compact, blazing furnaces.

71
00:04:36,784 --> 00:04:37,367
Step two.

72
00:04:37,367 --> 00:04:41,637
VY Canis Majoris, about fourteen hundred times wider than the Sun.

73
00:04:41,637 --> 00:04:47,784
In our solar system, its surface would swallow the orbit of Jupiter and stop partway to Saturn.

74
00:04:47,784 --> 00:04:49,272
And it's falling apart.

75
00:04:49,272 --> 00:04:58,331
Hubble images show this star wrapped in huge arcs and knots of gas, thrown off in giant outbursts, as if it can barely hold itself together.

76
00:04:59,031 --> 00:04:59,510
Our jet?

77
00:04:59,510 --> 00:05:02,442
About seven hundred and ninety years for one lap.

78
00:05:02,442 --> 00:05:07,947
And light itself, the fastest thing there is, would need almost six hours to circle it once.

79
00:05:07,947 --> 00:05:11,298
Light gets from the Sun to Earth in about eight minutes.

80
00:05:11,997 --> 00:05:13,140
So why stop there?

81
00:05:13,140 --> 00:05:16,695
Why isn't there a star as big as the whole solar system?

82
00:05:16,695 --> 00:05:18,917
Because every star is a tug of war.

83
00:05:18,917 --> 00:05:20,440
Gravity squeezes inward.

84
00:05:20,440 --> 00:05:23,106
Heat and light from the core push outward.

85
00:05:23,106 --> 00:05:33,072
Make a cool supergiant bigger and brighter, and its outer layers get so loosely held that they start drifting off into space, carried away by powerful winds.

86
00:05:33,072 --> 00:05:40,626
Studies of large samples of red supergiants point to a ceiling, somewhere around fifteen hundred times the Sun's width.

87
00:05:40,626 --> 00:05:43,863
Past that, the star simply can't hold on to itself.

88
00:05:44,563 --> 00:05:47,022
And yet, one star seems to break the rule.

89
00:05:47,022 --> 00:05:53,753
Stephenson two eighteen, nearly nineteen thousand light years away, hidden behind dust in the constellation Scutum.

90
00:05:53,753 --> 00:05:59,255
Some estimates put it at around two thousand one hundred and fifty times the width of the Sun.

91
00:05:59,255 --> 00:06:04,874
Drop that into our solar system, and its surface would reach all the way to the orbit of Saturn.

92
00:06:04,874 --> 00:06:07,625
You could fit about ten billion Suns inside it.

93
00:06:08,325 --> 00:06:11,961
Our jet would need about twelve hundred years to fly around it.

94
00:06:11,961 --> 00:06:13,982
Light would need almost nine hours.

95
00:06:13,982 --> 00:06:15,309
But remember the catch.

96
00:06:15,309 --> 00:06:22,178
That size rests on a distance and a brightness that are both uncertain, and it breaks the ceiling we just talked about.

97
00:06:22,178 --> 00:06:24,372
Many astronomers suspect it's smaller.

98
00:06:24,372 --> 00:06:27,258
So the honest answer to which star is the largest?

99
00:06:27,258 --> 00:06:28,527
We're still measuring.

100
00:06:29,227 --> 00:06:32,965
And the most dramatic story played out almost in real time.

101
00:06:32,965 --> 00:06:41,074
In twenty twenty four, ESO's Very Large Telescope Interferometer captured the first close-up image of a star outside our galaxy.

102
00:06:41,074 --> 00:06:47,473
WOH G sixty four, in the Large Magellanic Cloud, about a hundred and sixty thousand light years away.

103
00:06:47,473 --> 00:06:54,251
A red supergiant, around fifteen hundred times wider than the Sun, wrapped in an egg-shaped cocoon of dust.

104
00:06:54,951 --> 00:07:05,568
Then, in twenty twenty five, a team led by Gonzalo Muñoz Sánchez looked back at more than thirty years of observations, and published a surprise in Nature Astronomy.

105
00:07:05,568 --> 00:07:07,820
WOH G sixty four has been changing.

106
00:07:07,820 --> 00:07:11,165
Its surface got more than a thousand degrees hotter.

107
00:07:11,165 --> 00:07:13,546
Its color shifted from red to yellow.

108
00:07:13,546 --> 00:07:18,050
And its estimated width dropped to around eight hundred times the Sun.

109
00:07:18,050 --> 00:07:22,618
One of the biggest stars we knew may be shrinking in front of our eyes.

110
00:07:22,618 --> 00:07:25,835
Some think it's a step toward its final explosion.

111
00:07:25,835 --> 00:07:30,145
Not everyone agrees, and that's what makes it so exciting to watch.

112
00:07:30,845 --> 00:07:33,114
And here's the twist closest to home.

113
00:07:33,114 --> 00:07:35,567
Our own Sun will become a red giant too.

114
00:07:35,567 --> 00:07:42,005
In about five billion years, it's expected to swell up and swallow Mercury and Venus, and possibly Earth.

115
00:07:42,005 --> 00:07:46,788
But even then, it will only reach a couple of hundred times its current width.

116
00:07:46,788 --> 00:07:51,816
Betelgeuse, today, is already about three times bigger than our Sun will ever get.

117
00:07:52,516 --> 00:07:54,649
So, the largest star versus the Sun?

118
00:07:54,649 --> 00:08:01,520
Somewhere between eight hundred and two thousand times wider, depending on which giant you trust, and when you look.

119
00:08:01,520 --> 00:08:03,297
Big enough to swallow Jupiter.

120
00:08:03,297 --> 00:08:05,370
Thin enough to be emptier than air.

121
00:08:05,370 --> 00:08:08,865
And restless enough to change size within a human lifetime.

122
00:08:08,865 --> 00:08:12,774
But you don't need a giant star to feel the scale of the universe.

123
00:08:12,774 --> 00:08:19,053
Right now, the ground under your feet is spinning at up to sixteen hundred and seventy kilometers an hour.

124
00:08:19,053 --> 00:08:21,837
So what would happen if Earth suddenly stopped?

125
00:08:21,837 --> 00:08:22,547
That's next.
